(((window.HALCYON_DATA=window.HALCYON_DATA||{}).asep=window.HALCYON_DATA.asep||{}).full=window.HALCYON_DATA.asep.full||[]).push({
  num: 5, part: `Part One: Concept`, title: `The Trade`, weeks: `Weeks 9 to 11`,
  tasks: [`M3`,`M4`,`M7`,`T3`,`T5`,`T6`,`X2`,`X3`,`O3`,`O4`],
  cast: [],
  opening: [
    `Once a month Amara Osei stood on a wooden shipping crate in the high bay and talked to the whole company. The crate had come with the factory. Somebody had stenciled CENTENNIAL LIGHTING on its side a long time ago, and it was the only thing in the building tall enough to stand on that wasn't on a work order.`,
    `On Monday of week ten about four hundred people stood around it in their jackets, because the heaters in the high bay didn't catch up until noon. A few of them were standing on Tiago's blue tape without noticing. Amara read the new hires first, every name, and waited for each one to wave.`,
    `Then she did the numbers. HEARTH was on budget. HEARTH was on schedule. Halo was happy. She didn't mention the rack. Pavel hadn't written back. She said it plainly and stepped down, and people clapped the way people clap for good news at nine in the morning.`,
    `She found Nadia by the coffee urn before the crowd had thinned. <span class="said" data-who="Amara">"I've got a number in my head for what the egress fix costs,"</span> she said. <span class="said" data-who="Amara">"Tell me yours is smaller."</span>`,
    `<span class="said" data-who="Nadia">"I don't have one yet. That's what the trade is for."</span>`,
    `Amara looked back at the crate. <span class="said" data-who="Amara">"We bid a firm fixed price. I signed it. Every dollar this costs past the bid comes out of FRO, not Halo."</span> She tipped her cup at the room, at the jackets heading back to their desks. <span class="said" data-who="Amara">"If it eats the margin, I'm the one who gets back up on that box and tells them."</span>`,
    `She went to her ten o'clock. Nadia stayed by the urn and looked down the outline on the floor, at the red X by the hatch, and then at the end of the tape where there wasn't one.`
  ],
  lesson: {
    title: `Colleen's Flight Notes: decide on purpose`,
    sections: [
      { h: `Three airplanes and a legal pad`,
        body: [`Colleen was buying an airplane. She'd borrowed one for two years and finally admitted it, and on Saturday she met Nadia at a hangar at the airport in Erie, where a broker had three used four seaters lined up nose to tail. She had a yellow legal pad. Down the left side she'd written what she cared about. Across the top were three tail numbers.`,
          `<span class="said" data-who="Colleen">"I already know which one I want,"</span> she said, and nodded at the oldest one, a taildragger with faded red trim. <span class="said" data-who="Colleen">"That's why I wrote the list first."</span>`,
          `Decision management is the process for making a choice on purpose. You identify the alternatives, describe them, evaluate them in a structured way, and pick the one that does the program the most good. Any process can raise a decision: an analysis turns up a problem, a review asks a question, a risk needs handling. The work comes in three parts. Prepare: state the decision, who makes it, by when, and how. Analyze: set the criteria, gather the alternatives, and evaluate each one with data, much of it from system analysis. Make and manage: choose, record the choice with its rationale and assumptions, and tell the people and processes that depend on it. A trade study is the usual form this takes on a technical question.`],
        terms: [[`Decision management`,`The process that gives decisions a structured, analytical basis: identify and evaluate alternatives against criteria, select the most beneficial, and record why.`],[`Trade study`,`A structured comparison of alternatives against weighted criteria, used to support a decision.`]],
        exam: `Order wins on decision questions. Define the decision and the criteria, then evaluate, then choose. An answer that picks first and justifies after is the wrong one.` },
      { h: `Musts, wants and weights`,
        body: [`Two lines sat in a box at the top of Colleen's pad: useful load with full fuel for her and two passengers, and an annual inspection with no open airworthiness items. <span class="said" data-who="Colleen">"Those don't get scored,"</span> she said. <span class="said" data-who="Colleen">"An airplane that can't carry us isn't a low score. It's a no."</span>`,
          `Criteria come in two kinds. Musts are thresholds, often taken straight from the requirements, and an alternative that fails one is screened out before anything is scored. Wants are what you trade, and each gets a weight that says how much it counts against the others. Set the weights before you score, with the stakeholders who own the concerns, and write down why. Weights chosen after the scores are visible drift toward whatever somebody already liked. Keep the criteria independent, so one concern isn't counted twice under two names. Then score each alternative on each want, on a common scale and from data where you can, multiply by the weight, and add. That's a weighted sum. A value function turns raw values like seconds or dollars into scores on the common scale, so the conversion is written down instead of guessed.`,
          `The taildragger lost. It was cheap and beautiful and it had the worst useful load of the three. <span class="said" data-who="Colleen">"Good,"</span> Colleen said, and didn't sound like she meant it.`],
        terms: [[`Must criterion`,`A threshold every alternative has to meet. Failing one screens the alternative out before scoring.`],[`Weighted sum`,`Each alternative's score on each criterion, times that criterion's weight, added up.`]],
        exam: `An option that fails a requirement threshold is out before scoring. Weights come from the stakeholders and are set before the alternatives are scored.` },
      { h: `How sure is sure`,
        body: [`Then she asked herself how much more she'd have to care about price for the taildragger to win, and worked it out in the margin. That's sensitivity analysis: change the weights or scores across a reasonable range and watch whether the winner changes. If it holds, the decision is robust. If a small change flips it, the decision is close, and whoever makes it needs to know exactly which judgment it rests on. <span class="said" data-who="Colleen">"I'd have to care about money twice as much as I do,"</span> she said. <span class="said" data-who="Colleen">"I might, in April. Not that much."</span>`,
          `The scores come from system analysis, and on a module some of them come from simulation, which is digital engineering earning its keep: a model can run fifty layouts in the time it takes to tape one. A simulation is only worth its inputs. Anchor it to measured data, check it against a case you already know, match its detail to the question, and list what it leaves out.`,
          `Two ideas from the quality characteristics part of the handbook live inside most trades. Affordability asks whether the system's life cycle cost fits what the stakeholders can pay while it still meets their needs. Value engineering is a method for getting there: break an expensive alternative into the functions it performs, and look for a cheaper way to perform each one without giving up performance, reliability or safety. It cuts cost. It doesn't cut function.`],
        terms: [[`Sensitivity analysis`,`Varying weights or scores to see whether the preferred alternative changes, which shows how robust the decision is.`],[`Value engineering`,`A method that keeps the required functions and finds the lowest life cycle cost way to perform them.`],[`Affordability`,`Whether a system's life cycle cost fits the stakeholders' budget while it still meets their needs.`]],
        exam: `Sensitivity analysis tests robustness. Value engineering keeps the function and lowers the cost. Removing the function is descoping, not value engineering.` },
      { h: `After you pick`,
        body: [`A decision record holds the problem, the alternatives, the criteria and weights with their reasons, the scores and where each came from, the sensitivity results, the assumptions, who decided and when, and any dissent. Then the decision goes to work. It may change system requirements or add derived ones, traced to the decision. Design definition takes the chosen alternative and works out the detailed characteristics needed to build it. And a choice usually creates risks of its own, which go on the register with owners like any other risk. Measurement keeps the decision honest afterward. It starts from an information need, what the decision makers have to know, and picks measures that answer it, such as predicted egress time tracked against its threshold.`,
          `FRO had no one who could model smoke, so the program brought in a fire protection engineer on contract for the trade. Human resource management covers that too: acquiring the skills a project needs when the organization doesn't have them, as well as growing the ones it does.`,
          `On the walk back to the cars Nadia told Colleen about the crate. <span class="said" data-who="Colleen">"That's not a HEARTH question,"</span> Colleen said. <span class="said" data-who="Colleen">"That's a company question."</span> Portfolio management is how an organization decides which projects to start, keep funding, redirect or end, balanced against its strategy and what it can afford. HEARTH is most of FRO's portfolio. Amara's margin is what lets FRO bid the next job, and four hundred paychecks ride on that. The program chooses the fix. The portfolio has to carry it.`],
        terms: [[`Decision record`,`The documented decision with its alternatives, criteria, weights, data, sensitivity, assumptions, decision maker and rationale.`],[`Information need`,`What a decision maker has to know. In the measurement process it drives which measures are collected.`],[`Portfolio management`,`The organizational process that starts, sustains, redirects or ends projects to meet the organization's strategic goals.`]],
        exam: `A decision feeds other processes: derived requirements to system requirements definition, the chosen alternative to design definition, new risks to risk management. Measures come from information needs, not from whatever is easy to count.` }
    ]
  },
  scenes: [
    { id:`f5s1`, domain:`Technical management`, task:`M3`, title:`What we're deciding`,
      text:(s,all,G)=>[`At two that afternoon Wes, Amara, Tiago and Jun were in the HEARTH room under the boxed 133, and everyone already had an answer.`,`<span class="said" data-who="Wes">"Move three and four up by the hatch,"</span> Wes said. <span class="said" data-who="Wes">"Galley goes aft. Done."</span> Amara wanted the cheapest thing that cleared 120 seconds. Tiago said a better hatch would do more than any wall. Jun had a sketch of the back of the module he hadn't shown anyone. What does Nadia do first?`],
      opts:[
        {t:`Go with Wes's fix and have Jun start moving the quarters on paper today.`, s:0, d:{trust:4,conf:-2,health:-4},
          after:`The quarters start moving on paper that afternoon. On Wednesday Tiago's hatch idea comes back up, and so does Jun's sketch, and nobody can say why they lost, because they never ran.`,
          why:`Expert judgment is an input to a decision, not the decision. Picking before the options are even listed means the other alternatives never get evaluated.`},
        {t:`Write the decision on the board with who makes it and by when.`, s:3, best:true, d:{trust:2,conf:5,health:5},
          after:`She writes it across the top of the whiteboard: decide how HEARTH meets the egress times in scenarios E1 and E2. Amara decides, on Wes's recommendation, by Friday of week eleven. Under it go four options instead of two, because Tiago adds his hatch and Jun finally shows his sketch. Amara reads the date and nods.`,
          why:`Decision management starts with preparing: state the decision, who makes it, by when, and how. Arguing about fixes before the question is written means each person is answering a different question.`},
        {t:`Have Wes and Amara each make their case, then compare the two fixes.`, s:1, d:{trust:3,conf:1,health:0},
          after:`The comparison is fair to both of them. It's a choice between two people, though, and Tiago and Jun go back to their desks with their ideas.`,
          why:`Comparing alternatives is the right instinct. Comparing only the loudest two skips the step where you gather every reasonable alternative.`},
        {t:`Ask Helen which fix Halo would rather live with, and start from that.`, s:0, d:{trust:1,conf:-2,health:-2},
          after:`Helen writes back kindly that she'd like FRO to tell her, with numbers. That's what she's paying for.`,
          why:`Stakeholders help set the criteria and weights. Handing them the choice before any analysis skips the work the decision needs.`}
      ]},
    { id:`f5s2`, domain:`Technical management`, task:`M3`, title:`Musts and wants`,
      text:(s,all,G)=>[`By Tuesday there were four options on the board. A: revision C as drawn, with a better emergency procedure. B: a faster hatch, with a lower sill and two handholds, plus small layout changes. C: the faster hatch, with all four quarters moved forward and the galley and hygiene station moved aft. D: a second way out. Halo's next node carries a docking port for a crew vehicle, and Halo's assembly plan could berth it at HEARTH's aft end instead of on the core module: HEARTH first, then the node, then the first crew. A second hatch there would put quarters three and four a few steps from a door, with a lifeboat past it.`,`Jun had started the criteria list. It had the 120 second limit on it, and cost, and mass, and crew comfort, and "Halo likes it," all in one column.`],
      opts:[
        {t:`Put every criterion into one weighted list, the 120 second limit included.`, s:1, d:{trust:2,conf:1,health:0},
          after:`Everything's on the list. The 120 seconds gets a weight like any other line, which means an option can fail it and still win on cost.`,
          why:`A complete list is good. A requirement threshold inside the weighted sum can be outscored, and a must shouldn't be tradeable.`},
        {t:`Weight cost and schedule, and leave safety to the requirements already set.`, s:0, d:{trust:2,conf:-2,health:-4},
          after:`The cheapest option wins on paper before anyone asks how much margin it leaves the last crew member out.`,
          why:`Meeting a requirement is the floor. How far above the floor each option lands is exactly what a safety trade needs to weigh.`},
        {t:`Make the scenario times musts that screen options out, then weight the rest.`, s:3, best:true, d:{trust:3,conf:5,health:5},
          after:`The list splits in two. Musts: E1 and E2 met from every bunk. Wants: egress margin, smoke on the escape path, cost, schedule and mass. "Halo likes it" gets crossed out, because Halo's view goes into the weights. Option A will have to clear 120 like everyone else.`,
          why:`Musts are thresholds, usually from requirements, that screen alternatives out. Wants are traded and weighted. Keeping them apart stops a cheap option from buying its way past a safety limit.`},
        {t:`Give each option one overall score for how well it meets the requirements.`, s:0, d:{trust:1,conf:-1,health:-3},
          after:`Each option gets one number. When Amara asks why D scored a seven, Nadia can't say which part of the seven was safety and which was cost.`,
          why:`Criteria need to be separate and independent so the trade shows what's being given up for what. One blended score hides the trade.`}
      ]},
    { id:`f5s3`, domain:`Technical management`, task:`M3`, title:`Who sets the weights`,
      text:(s,all,G)=>[`Wednesday the weights came up, and the room got louder. Helen, on the screen from Halo, wanted smoke on the escape path to count for half. Amara wanted cost at thirty percent. Odette wanted egress margin over everything and said so in one sentence. Wes said weights were a way to make a gut call look like math.`,`<span class="said" data-who="Wes">"I've picked hatches for thirty years,"</span> he said. <span class="said" data-who="Wes">"Show me the four and I'll tell you."</span>`],
      opts:[
        {t:`Skip the weights and let the room pick from the four options side by side.`, s:0, flag:`unweightedtrade`, d:{trust:4,conf:-3,health:-6},
          after:`They argue for twenty minutes, mostly Wes, and agree they'll know it when they see it. What mattered never gets written down, so later, when somebody asks why, the answer is who was in the room.`,
          why:`Without stated criteria and weights a choice can't be explained, checked or defended. Experience belongs in the scores and the weights, where people can see it.`},
        {t:`Set the weights with Helen, Odette and Amara before anyone scores an option.`, s:3, best:true, d:{trust:3,conf:5,health:5},
          after:`It takes an hour. They land on thirty for egress margin, thirty for smoke on the path, twenty for cost, ten for schedule and ten for mass. Amara argues for thirty on cost and loses, and asks for that in the record. Wes says it's still a gut call. <span class="said" data-who="Odette">"Five of them,"</span> Odette says, <span class="said" data-who="Odette">"written down."</span>`,
          why:`Weights express how much each concern matters, so they come from the stakeholders who own those concerns, before any option is scored, with reasons recorded. Disagreement goes on the record too.`},
        {t:`Score the options first, then set the weights once the numbers show a leaning.`, s:0, d:{trust:2,conf:-2,health:-4},
          after:`The scores come in. Then the weights move, a little at a time, toward whichever option each person already liked.`,
          why:`Weights set after the scores are visible stop measuring importance and start measuring preference. Set them first.`},
        {t:`Have each stakeholder set weights alone, then average them into one set.`, s:1, d:{trust:3,conf:1,health:1},
          after:`The averages look fair. They also hide that Helen and Amara are thirty points apart on the same line, which is the conversation that needed to happen.`,
          why:`Getting every stakeholder's weights is right. Averaging them hides the disagreement the decision maker most needs to see.`}
      ]},
    { id:`f5s4`, domain:`Technical`, task:`T6`, title:`The door`,
      text:(s,all,G)=>{const a2=all&&all[2];const tr=!!(a2&&a2.picks&&a2.picks[11]===1);return [`Wednesday night Nadia was at her kitchen table with Jun's spreadsheet when her phone lit up. Callie. It was after ten, which meant nights.`,`<span class="said" data-who="Callie">"You called. Don't make it a thing."</span>`,`<span class="said" data-who="Nadia">"I'm not making it a thing."</span>`,`<span class="said" data-who="Callie">"You're making it a thing in your head. I can hear it."</span> Her crew was up a pole near Avondale swapping a transformer. She had twenty minutes. Nadia asked how she knew they were safe up there. <span class="said" data-who="Callie">"I know it's open at my switches. They test it before they touch it. When I climbed I did the same. Twice, with the right meter, where I'm touching. I got that in Dad's class."</span>`,`<span class="said" data-who="Nadia">"And if the meter's wrong?"</span>`,`<span class="said" data-who="Callie">"Then I've got two meters and a crew that isn't stupid."</span> A beat. <span class="said" data-who="Callie">"You do this. You call and then you check my work."</span>`,`<span class="said" data-who="Nadia">"I got it at the dinner table."</span>`,`<span class="said" data-who="Callie" data-tone="soft">"Yeah. I know you did."</span> Then, quicker: <span class="said" data-who="Callie">"He wants to see your module. He won't ask. Give him a day or he'll never come."</span> Nadia gave her Thursday of next week. Callie's radio went, and she was gone.`,`Nadia went back to the spreadsheet. Each option had one E2 time, and each had been worked out by the person who liked it. Wes's used the engineers' walking speed.`,(tr?`Tiago's used the slowest of Helen's trainers from week four.`:`Helen had sent three trainers on Tuesday to walk the tape lights out, so the trade would have real times. Tiago's used the best of them.`)]},
      opts:[
        {t:`Use each owner's own numbers, but mark on the chart where the assumptions differ.`, s:1, d:{trust:3,conf:1,health:0},
          after:`The chart is honest about the differences. It still compares four answers to four slightly different questions.`,
          why:`Flagging inconsistent assumptions is better than hiding them. Comparing alternatives fairly needs one set of assumptions for all of them.`},
        {t:`Rerun all four options on the times of the fastest of Helen's three trainers.`, s:0, d:{trust:2,conf:-2,health:-4},
          after:`Every option gets faster, and the margins look comfortable. They're margins for the most practiced walker, not the slowest one.`,
          why:`Analysis for a safety decision uses representative or conservative inputs, stated as assumptions. Best case inputs flatter every option.`},
        {t:`Run every option on the same trainer walk data and find what sets each total.`, s:3, best:true, d:{trust:4,conf:5,health:5},
          after:`On one set of assumptions the answer jumps out. The hatch sets the time: first arrival, plus fifteen seconds for each person through, plus closing. Moving the quarters barely helps, because everyone still queues at one door. The faster hatch, eleven seconds a person, gets B and C to 117. D, two through each hatch, has both closed by 105. Tiago reads it over her shoulder Thursday morning. <span class="said" data-who="Tiago">"It was the door. It was always the door."</span>`,
          why:`System analysis gives decision makers data they can compare: the same assumptions for every alternative, stated, and the drivers of the result found. Here the driver is hatch throughput, which no wall move changes.`},
        {t:`Average the owners' estimates into one figure for each of the four options.`, s:0, d:{trust:2,conf:-1,health:-3},
          after:`The averages are smooth, and wrong in ways that can't be traced, since each mixes three people's walking speeds.`,
          why:`Averaging inconsistent analyses doesn't remove the inconsistency. It hides it. Rerun them on common assumptions.`}
      ]},
    { id:`f5s5`, domain:`Cross-cutting`, task:`X3`, title:`Smoke and people`,
      text:(s,all,G)=>[`E2 was the leak. E1 was the fire, and fire needed smoke, which FRO had never modeled. Odette found a fire protection engineer she'd worked with before, and Amara signed her on for three weeks.`,`By Friday Jun had the engineer's smoke model joined to his own crew model: a food warmer fire at the galley, smoke moving with the cabin air, and four people walking from their bunks at speeds Jun typed in from the tape. On the screen they were four blue dots. It looked finished. She'd signed enough sheets to know what that was worth.`],
      opts:[
        {t:`Run it on the engineer's standard settings, the ones she uses on buildings.`, s:1, d:{trust:3,conf:1,health:0},
          after:`The settings are sound for buildings. HEARTH has no stairwells and no doors, and its fans move air the whole time.`,
          why:`A tool proven elsewhere is a good start. A model is only valid for the conditions it's been checked against, so check it against this one.`},
        {t:`Test the model against the trainer walks and a published galley fire test.`, s:3, best:true, d:{trust:4,conf:5,health:5},
          after:`Jun reruns the trainer walks as a test. The dots land within two seconds of Tiago's stopwatch. The engineer checks the smoke against a published galley fire test and it tracks. Then they write the assumptions page, nine lines. The first one says the cabin is modeled as one open volume with the fans at nominal flow.`,
          why:`Simulation earns trust when it's anchored to measured data, checked against a known case, matched in detail to the question, and honest about what it leaves out. The assumptions page belongs in the decision record.`},
        {t:`Model every detail of the cabin, down to the cable trays, before running it.`, s:0, d:{trust:1,conf:-1,health:-3},
          after:`Two weeks go into cable trays. The trade is due in one.`,
          why:`Fidelity should match the decision. More detail costs time and doesn't make a model right if it was never checked against data.`},
        {t:`Run it once on revision C and apply that smoke pattern to all four options.`, s:0, d:{trust:2,conf:-1,health:-3},
          after:`The pattern is revision C's. Options C and D change where people go, and the smoke goes somewhere else.`,
          why:`Each alternative has to be analyzed as itself. Results from one configuration don't carry over to a different layout.`}
      ]},
    { id:`f5s6`, domain:`Technical`, task:`T6`, title:`Nine seconds`,
      text:(s,all,G)=>[`Monday of week eleven Jun ran E1 on every option and put revision C up first, because it was the one they'd have to beat.`,`<prop type="doc" title="HEARTH smoke and egress simulation, E1 (galley fire), revision C as drawn">Alarm at 0 seconds. Galley aisle impassable at 70 seconds.<br>Quarters 1 and 2: walk away from the galley to the hatch.<br>Quarters 3 and 4, revision C as drawn: past the galley at 61 seconds. Margin 9 seconds.<br>Same run, seat track on one side only: past the galley at 52 seconds. Margin 18 seconds.<br>Assumptions: cabin modeled as one open volume, fans at nominal flow.</prop>`,`The numbers came out clean, every row filled. She felt the small click she used to get from a green PASS, and let herself have it.`,`Wes was standing at the back. He didn't say anything. Jun's numbers were the averages of the trainers' runs, and the trainers had varied by six seconds either way.`],
      opts:[
        {t:`Report each option's best run, the one from the most practiced trainer.`, s:0, d:{trust:2,conf:-2,health:-4},
          after:`The best runs are the trainers who'd done it four times. Every margin looks generous.`,
          why:`Best case results show the design's ceiling. A safety decision needs to know how bad it gets, not how good.`},
        {t:`Report each margin with its spread, the slowest trainer's run included.`, s:3, best:true, d:{trust:4,conf:5,health:5},
          after:`With the slowest trainer, B's margin at the galley drops from eighteen seconds to twelve. D's hardly moves, because in D quarters three and four walk away from the galley. Jun writes the driver under each one. For B it's walking speed past the fire. For D it's the hatch.`,
          why:`System analysis reports results with their uncertainty and the factors driving it, so the decision maker can see which options are close to a limit and why.`},
        {t:`Report each option's average run, with a note that real crews will vary.`, s:1, d:{trust:3,conf:1,health:0},
          after:`The averages are honest. The note doesn't say by how much, and B's margin, the thin one, looks as solid as D's.`,
          why:`An average is a fair center. Without the spread, an option close to its limit looks as safe as one far from it.`},
        {t:`Hold every result until the engineer reruns it all at a finer resolution.`, s:0, d:{trust:1,conf:-1,health:-3},
          after:`The finer runs take a week. They move every number by under a second.`,
          why:`Analysis supports a decision on a schedule. When the uncertainty is understood and stated, more resolution often changes nothing that matters.`}
      ]},
    { id:`f5s7`, domain:`Technical management`, task:`M3`, title:`Turning the weights`,
      text:(s,all,G)=>{const gut=(s.flags&&s.flags.unweightedtrade)||G.unweightedtrade;
        return [`Tuesday morning there was a print on Nadia's chair. Revision D. Note eight now read SEAT TRACK ONE SIDE OF AISLE ONLY, and the aisle was back to the width of the tape. Wes had initialed it.`,
        (G.acceptedwesreqs
          ?`Note eight was a line in the baseline too, with no parent, because he'd put it there. Under the print was a change request to take his own line out, filled in, in his own block letters.`
          :`Clipped to it was a change request in his own block letters, with the rationale already filled in.`),
        `When she went to find him he was on the phone with structures. He held up one hand without turning around. <span class="said" data-who="Wes">"Nine seconds,"</span> he said, to her or to the phone. <span class="said" data-who="Wes">"Cargo never had to run."</span>`,
        (gut
          ?`Jun had built a matrix anyway, with weights he'd picked himself on Thursday night so the columns would add up.`
          :`Jun had the matrix up by ten, with Wednesday's weights.`),
        `<prop type="chart" kind="bars" title="HEARTH egress trade, weighted scores out of 10" labels="B faster hatch,C quarters forward,D second path" values="5.6,5.5,7.0">Weights: egress margin 0.30, smoke on the path 0.30, cost 0.20, schedule 0.10, mass 0.10. Option A screened out: E2 at 133 seconds.</prop>`,
        `Amara looked at the bars for a while. <span class="said" data-who="Amara">"Run it with cost at forty,"</span> she said.`]},
      opts:[
        {t:`Rerun it with cost at forty, and show her both rankings side by side.`, s:1, d:{trust:3,conf:1,health:1},
          after:`At forty, B wins. Amara now has two answers and no idea where one turns into the other.`,
          why:`Testing a stakeholder's weights is part of sensitivity analysis. Two points don't show where the decision changes, which is what she needs.`},
        {t:`Keep the agreed weights and tell her a change would reopen the trade.`, s:0, d:{trust:-4,conf:0,health:-1},
          after:`Amara doesn't push. She also stops trusting the matrix, and that matters more on Friday than the matrix does.`,
          why:`Agreed weights are the baseline, not a wall. Sensitivity analysis exists to show how the result moves when the judgments do.`},
        {t:`Run cost from twenty to forty percent and show her where it flips.`, s:3, best:true, d:{trust:4,conf:5,health:5},
          after:`Taking the extra weight from smoke, D holds with cost at thirty. Somewhere near thirty three, B passes it. Nadia puts the crossing on one slide. Amara studies it. <span class="said" data-who="Amara">"So it comes down to whether money is a third of this."</span> <span class="said" data-who="Nadia">"Whether you and Halo agree it is,"</span> Nadia says. Her voice holds. Amara notices that too.`,
          why:`Sensitivity analysis varies weights across a reasonable range and finds where the preferred option changes. Showing the crossover tells the decision maker exactly which judgment the choice rests on.`},
        {t:`Report the agreed result only, and keep the cost flip out of the deck.`, s:0, d:{trust:1,conf:-3,health:-3},
          after:`The slide is clean. In a month Amara runs it herself, finds the flip, and wonders what else was left off.`,
          why:`Hiding a result that cuts against your recommendation isn't analysis, it's advocacy. Decision makers need the robustness of the answer, good or bad.`}
      ]},
    { id:`f5s8`, domain:`Cross-cutting`, task:`X2`, title:`Same door, less money`,
      text:(s,all,G)=>[`D came in at four point six million dollars and six weeks. Amara's reserve for all of development had been eleven million when she signed.`,`<span class="said" data-who="Amara">"If it's D, it's D,"</span> she said on Wednesday. <span class="said" data-who="Amara">"Make it cost less."</span> Wes had already priced a custom berthing ring for the aft end and a new hatch to go in it.`],
      opts:[
        {t:`Save on the aft hatch with one latch in place of the forward hatch's two.`, s:0, d:{trust:2,conf:-2,health:-5},
          after:`It saves money and takes away the second latch the hazard report counts on. Odette notices in a day.`,
          why:`Removing a function or a safety feature to save money isn't value engineering. It changes what the system does.`},
        {t:`Ask Wes for the cheapest aft hatch he can build, and take what it can do.`, s:1, d:{trust:3,conf:1,health:0},
          after:`Wes builds cheap well. Whether the cheap hatch still passes a person in eleven seconds is a question he wasn't asked.`,
          why:`Asking for lower cost is fair. Value engineering holds the required function fixed and asks what it costs, not the other way around.`},
        {t:`List D's functions with Wes and find a cheaper way to do each, losing none.`, s:3, best:true, d:{trust:3,conf:5,health:5},
          after:`Jun and Wes list them: open, pass a person, seal, hold pressure, berth. The aft hatch becomes a copy of the forward hatch, designed once and built twice. The custom ring becomes Halo's standard passive ring, which Halo already qualifies. D drops to three point eight million. The crew lose nothing.`,
          why:`Value engineering analyzes functions and finds the lowest life cycle cost way to perform each one, without lowering performance, reliability or safety. Affordability is the goal it serves.`},
        {t:`Leave cost alone until design is finished and the real numbers are known.`, s:0, d:{trust:1,conf:-1,health:-3},
          after:`By the end of design, the custom ring has drawings, tooling and a supplier, and it costs what it costs.`,
          why:`Cost is cheapest to change early. Waiting for design to finish locks in the expensive choices.`}
      ]},
    { id:`f5s9`, domain:`Technical management`, task:`M4`, title:`Who checks these`,
      text:(s,all,G)=>[`Thursday Frank drove up from Pueblo and was in the lot at eight, because he'd left at six to beat traffic that wasn't there. He wore his work jacket zipped to the collar, though the high bay was warm by then.`,`Tiago met them at the door. Frank shook his hand with his right. His left stayed in his jacket pocket, and it stayed there the whole tour. She'd meant to show him the model. He wanted the tape. At the end of it he stood in quarter four's square a while, the left hand still in the pocket. <span class="said" data-who="Frank">"That's the long way."</span>`,`<span class="said" data-who="Nadia">"That's the one I'm fixing."</span>`,`He looked at her instead of the tape, a beat past how he usually looked at anybody. Then they went out to the pressure test cell.`,`At the cell he stopped. Six red lockout tags hung on the valve panel, each with a name and a date. He read every one, bending for the ones down low.`,`<span class="said" data-who="Frank">"Who checks these?"</span>`,`<span class="said" data-who="Tiago">"I do,"</span> Tiago said. <span class="said" data-who="Tiago">"Then the person whose name is on it checks again. Only they take it off."</span> Frank nodded and touched the nearest tag with his right hand, squaring it on its wire.`,`In the lot he said it was a clean shop. She waited for the rest of it. There wasn't any. After his truck was gone she went back in and read the six tags herself.`,`That afternoon Helen wrote that Halo would work toward berthing its node at HEARTH's aft end before the first crew moved in. Work toward. If it slipped, she wrote, Halo would fly three until it berthed, and she'd be the one who told the fourth.`],
      opts:[
        {t:`Note it as an assumption in the trade record, to recheck at design review.`, s:1, d:{trust:2,conf:1,health:0},
          after:`It's written down. An assumption doesn't have an owner or a trigger, so nothing is watching for the day it stops being true.`,
          why:`Recording the assumption is better than nothing. A dependency that could defeat the chosen option is a risk and needs managing as one.`},
        {t:`Leave it off HEARTH's register as a risk that belongs to Halo's node team.`, s:0, d:{trust:3,conf:-2,health:-5},
          after:`The node is Halo's. The second hatch opening onto nothing would be HEARTH's.`,
          why:`A risk to your system's ability to meet its needs is yours to manage, even when the cause sits with someone else.`},
        {t:`Put it on HEARTH's register with Nadia as owner and Halo's plan as the trigger.`, s:3, best:true, d:{trust:4,conf:5,health:5},
          after:`Nadia owns it. The trigger is Halo's next assembly plan not showing the node. Helen's fallback goes in as the contingency, signed.`,
          why:`Risk management identifies the risks a decision creates, analyzes them, and plans the handling: here, mitigation through a written commitment and a contingency with a trigger, all with an owner.`},
        {t:`Take D off the board and decide among the options that don't need the node.`, s:0, d:{trust:1,conf:-2,health:-3},
          after:`Odette says no before Amara can. D stays on the board, the node still isn't on the register, and now Odette knows you wanted it gone.`,
          why:`Every option carries risk. Handle the risk and keep the decision on its criteria, rather than rejecting an option because a risk exists.`}
      ]},
    { id:`f5s10`, domain:`Technical management`, task:`M3`, title:`Write it down`,
      text:(s,all,G)=>[`Amara would decide at nine on Friday. Thursday night the HEARTH room emptied out around Nadia until it was her, the 133 in its red box, and a blank decision record.`,`Wes stuck his head in on his way out. <span class="said" data-who="Wes">"One page,"</span> he said. <span class="said" data-who="Wes">"Nobody reads two."</span>`],
      opts:[
        {t:`Write a record a stranger could check, with the reason for each number.`, s:3, best:true, d:{trust:3,conf:5,health:5},
          after:`It runs a page and a half. The assumptions get their own block, nine lines from the model and one from Helen. Amara's thirty percent goes in under dissent with her name on it. Wes reads the first page and turns it over to see the half.`,
          why:`A decision record captures what was decided and why: alternatives, criteria and weights with reasons, data and sources, sensitivity, assumptions, the decision maker, and dissent. It's what lets anyone check or revisit it later.`},
        {t:`Record the decision and its final scores, with the whole matrix attached.`, s:1, d:{trust:3,conf:1,health:1},
          after:`The scores are there. Why the weights were what they were, and what the model assumed, are not.`,
          why:`Scores are part of a record. Without the reasons for the weights and the assumptions behind the data, no one can tell later whether the decision still holds.`},
        {t:`Let the minutes from Friday's meeting serve as the record of the decision.`, s:0, d:{trust:2,conf:-2,health:-3},
          after:`The minutes say D was chosen. Eight months later a new engineer asks why, and the answer is in four people's heads.`,
          why:`Minutes record that a meeting happened. A decision record has to stand on its own, long after the people in the room have moved on.`},
        {t:`Record the winning option and its cost, with the rest kept in Jun's model.`, s:0, d:{trust:1,conf:-1,health:-3},
          after:`The cost is easy to find. The rest is somewhere in a model only Jun opens.`,
          why:`Rationale filed out of sight isn't part of the record. Keep the decision and its reasons together under information management.`}
      ]},
    { id:`f5s11`, domain:`Technical management`, task:`M7`, title:`What to watch`,
      text:(s,all,G)=>{const cost=(s.picks&&s.picks[7]===2)?`three point eight`:`four point six`;return [`At nine on Friday Amara chose D, the second egress path, and signed the bottom of the page. She looked at the schedule line a while and didn't say anything about it.`,`Then she capped her pen. <span class="said" data-who="Amara">"If I'm spending ${cost} million on this, I want to know every week whether it's still working. What do I look at?"</span>`]},
      opts:[
        {t:`Send her a weekly report with all the model and schedule can produce.`, s:0, d:{trust:2,conf:-1,health:-3},
          after:`The first weekly report is forty pages. Amara reads the first one and stops opening them.`,
          why:`Measuring everything answers no one's question. Measures are chosen to meet specific information needs.`},
        {t:`Find out what Amara needs to see each week, then pick measures for it.`, s:3, best:true, d:{trust:4,conf:5,health:5},
          after:`They need three things: is the crew still getting out in time, is the fix still on budget, and is Halo's node still coming. So, predicted E1 and E2 margins as technical performance measures against their thresholds, cost to complete on D, and the node risk's status. It fits on a card. Amara puts it in her pocket.`,
          why:`The measurement process starts with the decision makers' information needs, then selects, collects, analyzes and reports measures that answer them. Fewer, better measures get used.`},
        {t:`Wait for the design reviews to show whether the fix is still working.`, s:0, d:{trust:2,conf:-2,health:-3},
          after:`The next design review is months out. Anything that drifts until then drifts unseen.`,
          why:`Reviews are snapshots. Measurement gives a steady view between them, so problems surface while they're cheap.`},
        {t:`Track the percent of D's design tasks finished each week against the plan.`, s:1, d:{trust:3,conf:1,health:0},
          after:`Progress is a real measure, and Amara likes it. It can read a hundred percent on a design that's lost its margin.`,
          why:`Progress against plan is useful. It doesn't answer the main need here, whether the fix still gets the crew out in time.`}
      ]},
    { id:`f5s12`, domain:`Technical`, task:`T5`, title:`Into the drawing`,
      text:(s,all,G)=>[`Friday afternoon Wes came by with a roll under his arm. He'd started the aft hatch from the forward hatch drawings, copied onto new sheets with AFT in the title block.`,`<span class="said" data-who="Wes">"It's the front door with a new name,"</span> he said. <span class="said" data-who="Wes">"I can have these out Monday."</span>`],
      opts:[
        {t:`Let Wes release the copied drawings Monday, with AFT in the title block.`, s:0, d:{trust:4,conf:-2,health:-4},
          after:`The aft hatch is released Monday. The aft end carries different berthing loads, and they aren't written down anywhere.`,
          why:`Reusing a design is fine once it's checked against the new element's requirements and conditions. Releasing it before that skips design definition.`},
        {t:`Hold the drawings until Halo's node team sends its complete interface design.`, s:0, d:{trust:1,conf:-1,health:-3},
          after:`Halo's team is three months from that. The aft end waits with them.`,
          why:`Design definition works with the interfaces as they're defined and matures with them. Waiting for the other side to finish first stalls both.`},
        {t:`Check the forward design against the aft hatch's own requirements and loads.`, s:3, best:true, d:{trust:3,conf:5,health:5},
          after:`Jun adds two derived requirements to the system set by change request, traced to the decision: the aft hatch's open time and its pass time. Wes checks the forward design against the aft end's loads and finds one bracket to thicken. The berthing interface with Halo's node goes on the interface list with two owners.`,
          why:`Design definition turns the chosen alternative into the detail needed to build it, consistent with its allocated requirements and interfaces. Reuse works when it's checked against the new conditions.`},
        {t:`Use the forward drawings, and log the aft loads as an item to check at PDR.`, s:1, d:{trust:3,conf:1,health:1},
          after:`The reuse is smart and the item is real. Tooling starts against a design that may need a thicker bracket, and PDR is months away.`,
          why:`Logging the open question is better than ignoring it. Checking the reused design against the new loads now is cheaper than finding out at the review.`}
      ]}
  ],
  drills: [
    { after:`f5s7`, title:`Weights and what if`, setup:[`Three options passed the screen. Each is scored 1 to 10 on five wants. Egress margin: B 4, C 4, D 9. Smoke on the path: B 3, C 8, D 9. Cost: B 9, C 5, D 4. Schedule: B 8, C 3, D 4. Mass: B 9, C 6, D 4.`,`Weights: egress margin 0.30, smoke 0.30, cost 0.20, schedule 0.10, mass 0.10. Weighted score is the sum of each score times its weight. For the sensitivity check, move 0.10 of weight from smoke to cost, so smoke is 0.20 and cost is 0.30.`],
      fields:[{label:`B weighted score`, answer:5.6, tol:0.01},{label:`C weighted score`, answer:5.5, tol:0.01},{label:`D weighted score`, answer:7.0, tol:0.01},{label:`D after the shift`, answer:6.5, tol:0.01},{label:`B after the shift`, answer:6.2, tol:0.01}],
      solution:[`B: 4 times 0.30 is 1.2, 3 times 0.30 is 0.9, 9 times 0.20 is 1.8, 8 times 0.10 is 0.8, 9 times 0.10 is 0.9. Total 5.6.`,`C: 1.2 + 2.4 + 1.0 + 0.3 + 0.6 = 5.5.`,`D: 2.7 + 2.7 + 0.8 + 0.4 + 0.4 = 7.0. D leads B by 1.4.`,`After the shift, D: 2.7 + 9 times 0.20, which is 1.8, + 4 times 0.30, which is 1.2, + 0.4 + 0.4 = 6.5. B: 1.2 + 0.6 + 2.7 + 0.8 + 0.9 = 6.2. D still leads, by 0.3.`,`Each 0.01 moved from smoke to cost adds 0.06 to B (9 minus 3) and takes 0.05 from D (4 minus 9), so the 1.4 gap closes 0.11 at a time. 1.4 divided by 0.11 is about 12.7, so the tie falls near a 0.127 shift, with cost weighted about 0.33. Amara's forty is past it: at a 0.20 shift B scores 6.8 and D 6.0.`,`That's the sensitivity result. D is the better choice unless cost is worth more than about a third of the decision, and that judgment, not the arithmetic, is what the decision maker owns.`,`If the extra weight comes from the other four in proportion instead, the tie moves out to cost at about 0.375. A sensitivity result has to say which weights gave up the difference.`] }
  ],
  exercise: { after:`f5s9`, title:`Which process is doing the work?`, intro:`A trade pulls in several processes at once. Pick the one each activity on the HEARTH trade belongs to.`,
    items:[
      {prompt:`Writing down that Amara decides, on Wes's recommendation, by Friday of week eleven.`, choices:[`System analysis`,`Decision management`,`Risk management`,`Measurement`], answer:1, why:`Preparing the decision, including who decides and by when, is the first part of decision management.`},
      {prompt:`Running every option's depressurization egress time on one set of walk data.`, choices:[`System analysis`,`Measurement`,`Decision management`,`Design definition`], answer:0, why:`Producing consistent data and information for a technical decision is system analysis.`},
      {prompt:`Logging Halo's node timing with an owner, a trigger and a fallback.`, choices:[`Decision management`,`Configuration management`,`Risk management`,`System analysis`], answer:2, why:`Identifying a risk the decision creates and planning how to handle it is risk management.`},
      {prompt:`Finding where the winner changes as the cost weight moves from 0.20 to 0.40.`, choices:[`Measurement`,`Risk management`,`Design definition`,`Decision management`], answer:3, why:`Sensitivity analysis on criteria weights is part of analyzing the decision information.`},
      {prompt:`Choosing predicted egress margin and cost to complete as the measures Amara will watch.`, choices:[`Measurement`,`System analysis`,`Decision management`,`Risk management`], answer:0, why:`Selecting measures from the decision makers' information needs is the measurement process.`},
      {prompt:`Working out the aft hatch's bracket thickness for the aft end's loads.`, choices:[`System analysis`,`Design definition`,`Implementation`,`Decision management`], answer:1, why:`Detailing an element enough to build it, consistent with its requirements and interfaces, is design definition.`}
    ]},
  quiz: [
    {q:`What is the main purpose of the decision management process?`, opts:[`Record the choices made at each technical review`,`Select the most beneficial option from evaluated ones`,`Give every decision to the most senior engineer`,`Keep design choices on file for a later audit`], a:1, why:`Decision management provides a structured, analytical way to identify and evaluate alternatives and select the most beneficial course of action, then record it.`, task:`M3`, domain:`Technical management`},
    {q:`A trade study has a requirement that crew egress take no more than 120 seconds. How should the trade use it?`, opts:[`As the most heavily weighted criterion`,`As one want traded among the others`,`As a must that screens alternatives out`,`As a tiebreaker when scores are close`], a:2, why:`Requirement thresholds are musts. An alternative that fails one is screened out before scoring, so it can't win on other criteria.`, task:`M3`, domain:`Technical management`},
    {q:`When should the criteria weights in a trade study be set?`, opts:[`Before any alternative has been scored`,`After scoring, so they fit the results`,`After the review, by the lead analyst`,`Once the preferred option is known`], a:0, why:`Weights reflect stakeholder priorities and are set before scoring, with reasons recorded. Weights set after the scores are visible drift toward a preferred answer.`, task:`M3`, domain:`Technical management`},
    {q:`A trade's preferred option changes when one weight moves by 0.03. What does this sensitivity result tell the decision maker?`, opts:[`The analysis was done wrong and must be redone`,`The top scoring option should be chosen anyway`,`The weights must be frozen so it can't change`,`The choice is close and rests on that weight`], a:3, why:`Sensitivity analysis shows robustness. A small change that flips the result means the decision is close, and the decision maker needs to know which judgment it turns on.`, task:`M3`, domain:`Technical management`},
    {q:`Besides the choice itself, what should a decision record capture?`, opts:[`Only the final weighted score of each option`,`Alternatives, criteria, rationale, assumptions`,`The names of everyone who attended the meeting`,`The hours spent on the trade study itself`], a:1, why:`A decision record holds the alternatives, criteria and weights with reasons, the data and sensitivity, the assumptions, the decision maker and any dissent, so the decision can be checked or revisited.`, task:`M3`, domain:`Technical management`},
    {q:`Each option in a trade was analyzed by its own supporter, using different input assumptions. What should system analysis do?`, opts:[`Keep each supporter's numbers as submitted`,`Average the results across the supporters`,`Rerun every option on common assumptions`,`Use the most optimistic inputs for all`], a:2, why:`System analysis gives decision makers comparable data: one stated set of assumptions for every alternative, with the drivers of the result identified.`, task:`T6`, domain:`Technical`},
    {q:`Why should a system analysis report the spread of its results and not just one value?`, opts:[`A range always makes the design look safer`,`Reviewers require at least two numbers`,`One value can't be traced to requirements`,`Decision makers need to know how sure it is`], a:3, why:`Analysis results go to decision makers with their uncertainty, so an option close to a limit isn't mistaken for one with plenty of margin.`, task:`T6`, domain:`Technical`},
    {q:`A team keeps every function of an expensive design but finds cheaper ways to perform each one. What method is this?`, opts:[`Value engineering`,`Reducing the scope`,`Configuration audit`,`Transferring risk`], a:0, why:`Value engineering analyzes functions and finds the lowest life cycle cost way to perform them without giving up performance, reliability or safety.`, task:`X2`, domain:`Cross-cutting`},
    {q:`As a quality characteristic, what does affordability weigh?`, opts:[`Purchase price against competitors' bids`,`Life cycle cost against needs and budget`,`Development cost against the fee earned`,`Unit cost against the production rate`], a:1, why:`Affordability asks whether the system's life cycle cost fits what stakeholders can pay while it still meets their needs.`, task:`X2`, domain:`Cross-cutting`},
    {q:`After a trade selects a second hatch, which process works out the detailed characteristics needed to build it?`, opts:[`System requirements definition`,`System architecture definition`,`Implementation`,`Design definition`], a:3, why:`Design definition provides the detailed data for each element, consistent with its allocated requirements and interfaces. Implementation then builds it.`, task:`T5`, domain:`Technical`},
    {q:`Before a smoke and egress simulation is used to compare layouts, what should the team do first?`, opts:[`Check it against measured data from a known case`,`Add every cabin detail to raise its fidelity`,`Run it once and reuse that run for every option`,`Accept the vendor's default settings as proven`], a:0, why:`A simulation earns trust by being anchored to measured data and checked against a known case, with detail matched to the question and its assumptions listed.`, task:`X3`, domain:`Cross-cutting`},
    {q:`In the measurement process, what should drive the choice of measures?`, opts:[`What the program's tools can collect`,`The customer's monthly report template`,`The decision makers' information needs`,`Benchmarks from other similar programs`], a:2, why:`Measurement starts by identifying information needs, then selects, collects, analyzes and reports measures that answer them.`, task:`M7`, domain:`Technical management`},
    {q:`A trade adds a second hatch, which now needs its own opening time limit. How is that limit best captured?`, opts:[`As a note on the hatch's design drawing`,`As a line in the trade's meeting minutes`,`As an item on the program risk register`,`As a derived system requirement, traced`], a:3, why:`Decision outcomes flow into system requirements definition. A new limit becomes a derived requirement, added through change control and traced to the decision that created it.`, task:`T3`, domain:`Technical`},
    {q:`A project needs a smoke modeling skill no one in the company has. Which process covers bringing that skill in?`, opts:[`Human resource management`,`Knowledge management`,`Portfolio management`,`Infrastructure management`], a:0, why:`Human resource management provides the people and competencies projects need, by developing them in house or acquiring them when the organization lacks them.`, task:`O4`, domain:`Agreement and organizational`},
    {q:`Which process decides which projects an organization starts, keeps funding, redirects or ends, in line with its strategy?`, opts:[`Project planning`,`Life cycle model management`,`Portfolio management`,`Decision management`], a:2, why:`Portfolio management starts and sustains the projects that meet the organization's strategic goals, and redirects or ends the ones that don't.`, task:`O3`, domain:`Agreement and organizational`}
  ],
  closing:(s,all,G)=>{G=G||{};const gut=(s.flags&&s.flags.unweightedtrade)||G.unweightedtrade;const pk=s.picks||{};const full=pk[2]===1&&pk[6]===2;const cost=pk[7]===2?`three point eight`:`four point six`;
    return [
    `Amara didn't wait for the first Monday of the month. She called the company into the high bay on the Monday after, and four hundred people came back out in their jackets, curious, because she never called one out of turn.`,
    `Over the weekend Tiago had laid a second red X at the end of the blue tape, past the fourth bunk. People kept looking down at it on their way to the crate.`,
    `Amara climbed up with an index card. Nadia could see it from the urn. The top line was the one from week ten, HEARTH ON BUDGET, ON SCHEDULE, with a line through it.`,
    `<span class="said" data-who="Amara">"HEARTH is going to be six weeks later than I told you,"</span> she said. <span class="said" data-who="Amara">"And ${cost} million dollars more than we bid for this piece of it. That comes out of us. I signed the price, so you're hearing it from me."</span>`,
    `Then she told them why. She told it the way Helen had, from the bunks. Four people, one door, a hundred and thirty three seconds. The last one out is whoever sleeps in the fourth bunk. She pointed at the second X. People at the back had to turn around to see it.`,
    (gut
      ?`Someone near the front asked how they'd decided. Amara didn't have an answer short enough for a crate. <span class="said" data-who="Amara">"We looked at all four. Wes came around to this one. So did I."</span> `+(pk[9]===0?`The weights on her record were Jun's, picked on a Thursday night, and Amara knew it.`:`Nadia had nothing on paper that said more than that.`)
      :(full
        ?`Someone near the front asked how they'd decided. Amara held up one sheet, the decision record. <span class="said" data-who="Amara">"Four options. Five things we cared about, weighted before anybody scored anything. It holds unless money is worth more than a third of this decision. I'm the one who signed the price, and I don't think it is."</span>`
        :`Someone near the front asked how they'd decided. Amara held up one sheet. <span class="said" data-who="Amara">"Four options. Five things we cared about, written down. D came out on top, and I'm the one who signed the price."</span>`)),
    (s.score>=28
      ?`Wes listened from the back of the outline, a step from the new X. Afterward he set his coffee on the concrete in the fourth bunk's square and walked to the X. Three steps. He paced it again to be sure, took his coffee and left.`
      :`Wes was at the back by the door. He clapped twice with everyone else and left before the crowd thinned. Revision D still had to be checked against the aft loads.`),
    `Amara folded the card. <span class="said" data-who="Amara">"I hate the six weeks,"</span> she said. <span class="said" data-who="Amara">"I'd do it again. Both of those are true. Go back to work."</span>`,
    `She stepped down. On her way past the urn she handed Nadia the card. Under the crossed out line, in the same pen, she'd written the new one: SIX WEEKS LATE. FOUR PEOPLE OUT.`
  ]},
  episode:{src:`audio/full-ch05.mp3`, len:`about 35 minutes`},
  next:`Tamarack`
});
